<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Émilie du Châtelet</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1706">1706</a>–<a href="/years/1749">1749</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Emilie du Chatelet, Madame du Châtelet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Émilie du Châtelet was a French mathematician and physicist. At Cirey, the du Châtelet château where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voltaire">Voltaire</a> settled in 1734, the two worked closely together and shared an interest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaac-newton">Isaac Newton</a>. Her Institutions de Physique (1740) discussed the ideas of both Newton and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottfried-wilhelm-leibniz">Leibniz</a>, and in the vis viva debate she repeated and publicised an experiment showing that kinetic energy is proportional to the square of velocity. Her French translation of Newton's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/principia-mathematica-newton">Principia</a>, with her commentary, completed in 1749 and published after her death, remains the standard French version.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emilie-du-chatelet.jpg" alt="Émilie du Châtelet" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maurice Quentin de La Tour · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emilie_Chatelet_portrait_by_Latour.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
